//
// A TRAP THAT DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY RESET.
//
// BACKLOG C4, `@X108`.  From the 2023 catalogue — *"spike trap: not
// automatically reset when activated"*, *"demolition: a triggered
// explosion, not automatically reset"* — and
// [`MATERIALS.md`](../docs/MATERIALS.md) § Towers is where it was
// ruled in: **this is `DESIGN.md` § What kind of game this is passed
// outright.**  A trap is placed in advance, fires once, and then
// somebody has to drive out to it, in the middle of a wave, to re-arm
// it.
//
// ⚠ It is *the tower-repair clock with the cost moved to the front*
// (`plans/17` T1).  A tower's servicing buys **thirty** shots and
// arrives at the END of its useful life; a trap's buys **one** blast
// and arrives at the START of every one.  That is the whole
// difference, and it is why a trap is a decision where a tower is an
// installation.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ The trigger is a CROSSING, never a standing position ─────
//
// The obvious implementation asks *who is standing on the plate at the
// end of the tick*, and it has a hole with a name: **the scout**.
// A tick is no longer a hex (`spawn.loft::enemy_tick`) — at 2.5 hex/s
// against a 0.667 s tick a scout is released **two** hexes in two ticks
// out of three, and the hex in the middle of a two-hex stride is one it
// is never standing on when a tick ends.  So a plate that asked about
// standing would be walked straight over by the one class fast enough
// to matter, roughly two crossings in five, and **no test using a
// regular robot could ever see it** — a regular takes exactly one hex
// per tick, so it stands on everything it crosses.
//
// That is `HARD_WON_RULES.md` § THE INSTRUMENT IS A CROSS-PRODUCT with
// the axis and the subject swapped once more, and the fix is to ask the
// right question rather than to sweep more often: `occupancy.loft`
// counts **visits** — every foot that has been set on a hex this tick,
// including the ones that were already standing there — and a trap
// fires on `occupancy_trodden`.  It costs one field and one line,
// because `Occupancy` is already threaded through the mover and
// `occupancy_enter` is already the ONE door every arrival goes through.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ The blast is a DISC of radius 1, and TWO derivations land
//        on that number ───────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **First: the trigger must not be able to out-stride its own blast.**
// A trap that hurt only its own hex would fire under a scout and then
// watch it walk out — the plate goes dark, the robot goes on, and the
// mechanic is a no-op for the class it most needs to stop.  A stride is
// at most two hexes (above), so a crosser ends **at most one hex** from
// the plate, and radius 1 catches it by construction.  ⚠ That is an
// INVARIANT and not an observation: the day a class strides three, the
// blast has to grow with it, and
// `tests/c4_the_trap.loft` § No class may out-stride the blast is what
// says so before a player finds out.
//
// **Second: it is `hex_disc_radius_1`** — the one footprint
// `numbers.json` names (§ tower.footprint_layout), the same disc
// `TOWER_REPAIR_REACH_HEXES` and `NIBBLE_REACH_HEXES` are each derived
// from.  Two arguments from opposite ends arriving at one number is
// what makes it a derivation rather than a tuning.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ Its real cost is the RAMP it builds, and nothing new was
//        written for that ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A trap that kills a cluster drops that cluster where it stood, and
// `DESIGN.md` § 7 already measured what a heap of bodies is worth to
// the people walking over it: **a tower without a cleanup crew makes a
// base fall SOONER — 104 ticks without it, 95 with** (plan 12 B7), because
// a robot climbs 2.0 m and three bodies on one hex is 1.5 m of ramp.
// A trap is that effect concentrated into one instant on one disc.
//
// So the design test is passed twice over.  A trap hands the player a
// burst of kills at a place they chose, and the kills become terrain
// somebody has to drive out and clear — at the worst possible moment —
// or the next wave walks over its own dead.  ⚠ **Which is why a trap
// at the wall face is not obviously the right place to put one**, and
// `tests/c4_the_trap.loft` measures that rather than asserting it.
//
// ── ⚠ It does not fire on the player, and the fiction says why ──
//
// `Occupancy` holds ENEMIES only (`occupancy.loft` § The other map), so
// a vehicle crossing a plate cannot trigger it — and that is the right
// answer rather than a convenient one: the player's vehicle **hovers**
// (`DESIGN.md` § Hover + boost), so there is no weight on the plate to
// set it off.  A trap that maimed the crew driving out to re-arm it
// would also make the one act the mechanic exists for unplayable.
//
// ── ⚠ The field stores SPENT, so [loft#914]'s default is ARMED ──
//
// A struct literal that omits a field takes that field's default
// SILENTLY, and a `boolean` defaults to `false`.  Stored as `spent`,
// an absent cell and a partial literal both read **armed**, which is
// what somebody who wrote `flag 4 0 trap` in a map meant.  Stored as
// `armed`, every trap anybody ever placed would be inert on arrival
// and nothing would be able to see it.
//
// Same argument `tower.loft` makes for `top_removed`, `damage.loft` for
// *taken*, `wallet.loft` for *spent* and `jammer.loft` for *off*:
// **the neutral value has to be what an author means.**
//
// ── What this does NOT decide ───────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠ **Buying one during a run.**  Plan 27 gives the player a tower by
// carrying a beacon out of the core; a trap beacon wants a second cargo
// kind and a key to choose between them, and `@X139` says the key table
// is a BUDGET that a new row needs an argument for.  So a trap is
// AUTHORED — which is exactly what *"placed in advance"* says — and the
// beacon route is left where plan 27 left it.
//
// ⚠ **Damaging the player's own walls.**  `DESIGN.md` § Damage TYPE
// gives splash that cost, and the whole table is designed-not-built
// (plan 12 B5a ships one untyped weapon).  Shipping one row of it here
// would make the trap's drawback read as a balance choice rather than
// as a TYPE, so the blast lands on robots only until the triangle
// arrives.
//
// ⚠ **Anything drawn in 3-D.**  The editor draws a trap marker
// (`marker_render.loft`); the play view's part-trees are
// `catalogue.loft`'s and adding a class there is plan 20's surface and
// the GL gate's.

use lattice;
use fixstep;

// ─── The numbers ────────────────────────────────────────────────

// What one blast lands on everything inside it.
//
// ⚠ **It is `damage.loft::ENEMY_REGULAR_HP`, and that is the
// definition rather than a figure**: a trap that fires once is only
// worth a trip if what it catches does not get up.  Deriving the damage
// from the robot's HP also says the right thing about a class that is
// tougher — a trap wounds a boss, it does not delete one.
//
// ⚠ **A LITERAL, and it has to be.**  A file-scope `const` initialised
// from a SIBLING MODULE's const panics the compiler through the
// aggregator ([loft#962]), so the relation is asserted at RUNTIME
// instead — `tests/c4_the_trap.loft` § The blast is a robot's whole HP
// is what stops the literal drifting.  Same shape as
// `tower.loft::TOWER_FOOTPRINT_M`.
pub const TRAP_DAMAGE: float = 30.0;   // = ENEMY_REGULAR_HP

// How far the blast reaches — see § The blast is a DISC of radius 1.
pub const TRAP_BLAST_HEXES: integer = 1;

// How close a vehicle has to be to work on a plate.
//
// ⚠ **DERIVED and not copied**, the same way `TOWER_REPAIR_REACH_HEXES`
// is: a trap occupies `hex_disc_radius_1`, so a vehicle within one hex
// is standing ON it.  It lands on the same number as the blast by
// arithmetic — both are that disc — and they are two constants because
// they are two facts, and the day a bigger charge has a wider blast the
// reach must not follow it.
pub const TRAP_REACH_HEXES: integer = 1;

// How long re-arming one takes, in the clock's base units.
//
// ⚠⚠ **There is no trap row in `numbers.json`, and this is CHOSEN.**
// It is the case BACKLOG C1's `scout` hit and `@X112` says to push back
// on: rather than invent a rate, re-arming takes the ONE servicing rate
// dryopea has — `tower.loft::TOWER_REPAIR_UNITS`, *"time for a helper
// to repair a black tower from scratch"*, 20 s.  A separate figure
// would be a tunable this work invented, which is the argument
// `tower.loft` already makes for using one rate for the player and for
// a helper.
//
// ⚠ **What that price MEANS is the whole of `@X108`.**  The same twenty
// seconds of standing buys thirty tower shots or one blast: the trap is
// an order of magnitude worse per second of servicing, and it is paid
// for by landing where you chose, at once, with no line of sight and no
// range — and by being re-armable while a tower that is still firing
// cannot be touched at all.
//
// ⚠ A LITERAL for `tower.loft::TOWER_REPAIR_UNITS`' reason — a const
// whose initialiser casts another computed const needs a temporary and
// loft refuses one in a const.  `tests/c4_the_trap.loft` § Re-arming is
// the servicing rate is what pins the two together.
pub const TRAP_REARM_UNITS: integer = 60000000;   // 20 s
pub const TRAP_REARM_SECONDS: float = (TRAP_REARM_UNITS as float)
                                      / (CLOCK_UNITS_PER_SECOND as float);

// ─── What a plate remembers between ticks ───────────────────────

// One trap plate: whether it has fired, and how far along its re-arm
// is.
//
// ⚠ **`spent` and NEVER `armed`** — see § The field stores SPENT.
//
// ⚠ `rearm` is a `fixstep::Timer` rather than a float of seconds, for
// plan 26 L3's reason: in integer base units there are no longer two
// directions to be unsafe in, and *armed* is a STATE the timer answers
// rather than a threshold somebody compares against zero.
pub struct TrapCharge {
    q:     integer,
    r:     integer,
    spent: boolean,
    rearm: Timer,
}

// Every plate on the map that has ever been touched.  A hash must be a
// struct field — the same loft idiom `TowerState`, `PaintedWorld` and
// `HeightLayer` follow.
//
// ⚠ **Absence is not the same as "no trap here".**  Whether a hex is a
// trap at ALL is the MARKER layer's question; this only says what a
// trap that exists has been through, which is why an unheard-of hex
// reads *armed* rather than *nothing*.
pub struct TrapState {
    cells: hash<TrapCharge[q, r]>,
}

// Factory: every plate armed, nobody working on anything.
pub fn trap_state_empty() -> TrapState {
    TrapState { cells: [] }
}

// Is the plate at (q, r) ready to fire?
//
// ⚠⚠ **Every reader asks THIS and never the field**, `jammer_on`'s rule
// for `jammer_off`: `spent` is stored so the silent default lands where
// an author means it, and `armed` is what the rules are written in.
//
// ⚠ **An absent cell answers TRUE**, which is the whole reason the
// field is spelled the way it is.
//
// Example: @DRY-125, @DRY-126
pub fn trap_armed(ts: const TrapState, q: integer, r: integer) -> boolean {
    ta_c = ts.cells[q, r];
    if ta_c == null { return true; }
    !ta_c.spent
}

// Fire the plate at (q, r).  Answers whether it went off — false when
// it had already been spent, which is what makes it fire ONCE.
//
// ⚠ It lands no damage: what a blast does to a roster is
// `spawn.loft::wave_traps`, because a roster is the wave engine's.
// This file owns what a trap IS and the tick owns what one DOES, the
// same split `tower.loft` and `spawn.loft` keep.
//
// ⚠ Firing CANCELS a re-arm in progress rather than leaving it banked.
// Nothing can reach that state today — a plate being re-armed is spent,
// and a spent plate does not fire — and the line is here so that a
// future half-armed plate cannot come back from a blast with the work
// somebody did on it still in the bank.
//
// Example: @DRY-125
pub fn trap_fire(ts: TrapState, q: integer, r: integer) -> boolean {
    tf_c = ts.cells[q, r];
    if tf_c == null {
        ts.cells[q, r] = TrapCharge { q: q, r: r, spent: true,
                                      rearm: timer_new() };
        return true;
    }
    if tf_c.spent { return false; }
    tf_c.spent = true;
    timer_cancel(tf_c.rearm);
    true
}

// Base time units banked toward (q, r)'s re-arm.  Zero for a plate
// nobody is working on.
//
// ⚠ The UP reading, `tower_repair_progress`' choice and for its reason:
// *time banked* rather than *time left*, so zero is the neutral value
// and one number answers both.
pub fn trap_rearm_progress(ts: const TrapState, q: integer, r: integer) -> integer {
    trp_c = ts.cells[q, r];
    if trp_c == null { return 0; }
    timer_spent(trp_c.rearm)
}

// Is somebody part-way through re-arming this plate?
//
// ⚠ It asks the TIMER rather than comparing its reading to zero — the
// same removal of an epsilon `tower_repairing` makes.
pub fn trap_rearming(ts: const TrapState, q: integer, r: integer) -> boolean {
    trg_c = ts.cells[q, r];
    if trg_c == null { return false; }
    timer_running(trg_c.rearm)
}

// Bank one turn of work into (q, r)'s re-arm.  Answers whether THIS
// call finished it.
//
// ⚠ **The progress lives on the PLATE, not on whoever is kneeling over
// it** — `tower_repair_tick`'s rule, and for the same reason: a crew
// member driven off at 19 of 20 seconds leaves the work where it
// stands, and the player finishes it.
//
// ⚠ An ARMED plate refuses the work outright, which is what stops a
// vehicle parked on a live trap banking twenty seconds of nothing and
// then re-arming it the instant it fires.
//
// ⚠ It ARMS on the first turn of work, so `trap_rearming` is a state
// rather than a comparison against zero.
//
// Example: @DRY-127
pub fn trap_rearm_tick(ts: TrapState, q: integer, r: integer,
                       elapsed: integer) -> boolean {
    if trap_armed(ts, q, r) { return false; }
    trt_c = ts.cells[q, r];
    // A spent plate HAS a cell by construction — `trap_armed` answers
    // true for an absent one and `trap_fire` creates one.  The guard is
    // here so the null case has an answer rather than because it is
    // reachable.
    if trt_c == null { return false; }

    if !timer_running(trt_c.rearm) { timer_arm(trt_c.rearm, TRAP_REARM_UNITS); }
    if !timer_spend(trt_c.rearm, elapsed) { return false; }

    trt_c.spent = false;
    true
}

// Is `at` inside the blast of a plate at `trap`?
//
// ⚠ `lat_distance` and nothing else — the rule `tower_in_range` carries,
// and the one moros#10 was: a `+ 1` on a `q` anywhere is the bug.
pub fn trap_in_blast(trap: const Hex, at: const Hex) -> boolean {
    lat_distance(trap, at) <= TRAP_BLAST_HEXES
}

// Is `at` close enough to work on the plate at `trap`?
pub fn trap_in_reach(trap: const Hex, at: const Hex) -> boolean {
    lat_distance(trap, at) <= TRAP_REACH_HEXES
}

// How many plates have been fired or worked on at least once.
//
// ⚠ Counts CELLS, so a map full of armed traps nobody has touched
// answers 0.  How many traps there ARE is the marker layer's count.
pub fn trap_count(ts: const TrapState) -> integer {
    len(ts.cells)
}
